window.docContents[151].resolve({"/UD-II___-expressions/Objects___-pointers-and-vectors/#Core-C______--UD-II___-expressions--Objects___-pointers-and-vectors":{"contents":"The literal nullptr has the internal type , compatible with every pointer type and with no other. Its value is .\n\nThe expression new C() allocates one location per field of , each with the default value of its type, then the record itself, tagged with the class, and evaluates to a pointer to the record. Its type is . The empty parentheses are the whole argument list until constructors arrive.\n\nThe expression new std::vector<τ>(n) evaluates the size, allocates one location per element with the default value of , then the vector record, and evaluates to a pointer to it. A negative size is error. The element type has values, so there are no vectors of objects, only of pointers to them.\n\nThe dereference *e denotes the location the pointer holds. When the pointer is  the result is error, where C++17 leaves the dereference undefined.\n\nThe field access e.f denotes the location of the field  in the record that  denotes, and e->f abbreviates (*e).f, so a null pointer is error. The type of the field comes from the class table.\n\nThe indexing e[i] denotes the location of element  of the vector  denotes. The vector is evaluated before the index, the order C++17 fixes for operator[]. An index outside  is error, where C++17 leaves it undefined. This is the completeness principle at work, a vector knows its size and every access is checked.\n\nReading *e, e.f, e->f or e[i] as a value is reading the content of the location it denotes, one rule for the four forms. The typing rules T-Deref, T-Field, T-Arrow and T-Index give the value the type of the location.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009UD II, expressions","header":"3.6. Objects, pointers and vectors","id":"/UD-II___-expressions/Objects___-pointers-and-vectors/#Core-C______--UD-II___-expressions--Objects___-pointers-and-vectors"},"/UD-II___-expressions/Unary-operators/#Core-C______--UD-II___-expressions--Unary-operators":{"contents":"Logical negation requires bool and arithmetic negation requires int. Negation goes through , because the negation of  overflows.In the code, the case Unary of Eval.expr evaluates the operand and calls Eval.unop.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009UD II, expressions","header":"3.3. Unary operators","id":"/UD-II___-expressions/Unary-operators/#Core-C______--UD-II___-expressions--Unary-operators"}});